        <section id="objects" >
          
            <h2>Objects</h2>
              <p>An object is a self-contained component that has methods and properties. So in our example:</p>
<ul>
<li>Class: Dogs</li>
<li>Attributes: Breed, Size, Age, and Colour</li>
<li>Methods: Eat(), Sleep(), Sit(), and Run()</li>
</ul>
<p><img src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u3_s2_p3_image4_2.svg" alt=""></p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="differences-between-class-and-object"  class="t-bg-supporting3">
          
            <h2>Differences between Class and Object</h2>
              <p>See below to see a table that outlines the differences between Class and Object.</p>

              
              
  <div class="toggle-group">
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    <h3>Class and Object</h3>
    <table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Class</th>
<th>Object</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>A class is a template for creating objects in program.</td>
<td>The object is an instance of a class.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>A class is a logical entity</td>
<td>Object is a physical entity</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>A class does not allocate memory space when it is created.</td>
<td>Object allocates memory space whenever they are created.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>You can declare class only once.</td>
<td>You can create more than one object using a class.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Example: Car.</td>
<td>Example: Jaguar, BMW, Tesla, etc.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Class generates objects</td>
<td>Objects provide life to the class.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Classes can’t be manipulated as they are not available in memory.</td>
<td>They can be manipulated.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>It doesn’t have any values which are associated with the fields.</td>
<td>Each and every object has its own values, which are associated with the fields.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>You can create class using “class” keyword.</td>
<td>You can create object using “new” keyword in Java</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

</toggle-section>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="attributes-and-properties"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Attributes and Properties</h2>
              <p>See below to learn about attributes and properties.</p>

              
              
  <div class="toggle-group">
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    <h3>Attributes</h3>
    <p>Attributes are the <em>data members</em> inside a class or an object.  Attributes may also be referred to as <em>fields</em> or <em>class variables</em> this will largely depend on which programming language is used. Attributes can be <em>Instance</em> and <em>Class</em> types.</p>
<p>So in our Objects Dog example the attributes for the <em>Class</em> Dog are: Breed, Size, Age, and Colour.</p>

</toggle-section>
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    <h3>Properties</h3>
    <p>A Property is a broad concept and used to determine a particular characteristic of a class. Properties are a way to attach custom behaviours to writing or reading a field.  Properties are an <em>abstraction</em>. It is a special sort of <em>class</em> and its functinality is somewhere between a <em>data member</em> and a <em>method</em>.</p>
<p>Properties are a special kind of attribute that provide <em>getter, setter</em> and <em>deleter</em> functionality to an attribute.</p>

</toggle-section>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
